      "locked": false,
      "text": "                                                                        LEADER ELECTION\nWhy You Need It\n\nMany distributed system components require exactly one \"leader\" to be active at a time:\n\n- Database primary: Exactly one node should accept writes to avoid conflicts\n- Kafka partition leader: Exactly one broker should handle produce/consume for each partition\n- Scheduled job coordinator: Exactly one instance should run the cron job (not all 10 instances)\n- Distributed lock manager: Exactly one node should grant locks at a time\n\nIf multiple nodes simultaneously believe they're the leader — split-brain — you get data corruption, duplicate job execution, or deadlocks.\n\nLEADER ELECTION is the algorithm by which distributed nodes agree on who is the leader, and detect when the leader fails so a new one can \nbe elected.\n\nREQUIREMENTS FOR A GOOD LEADER ELECTION ALGORITHM:\n\n- Safety: At most one leader at any time. Never two.\n- Liveness: Eventually, a leader is elected (the system doesn't get stuck)\n- Fault tolerance: If the leader fails, a new leader is elected\n- Freshness: The newly elected leader has the most up-to-date state (so it doesn't undo committed writes)\n\nThese requirements create tension. Safety and liveness are in tension during partitions (CAP theorem again). A system that ensures at most one \nleader (safety) might sacrifice liveness during partitions. A system that always elects a leader (liveness) might allow split-brain (sacrificing safety).\n\n                                                            THE RAFT ALGORITHM — HOW IT WORKS\n\nRaft is the dominant modern leader election algorithm. It powers etcd (Kubernetes' state store), CockroachDB, TiKV, and many others. It was \ndesigned specifically to be understandable (unlike Paxos, which is notoriously hard to understand).\n\nRaft has three node states:\n\nFollower: Default state. Follows the leader's instructions. If it doesn't hear from a leader for an election timeout (150-300ms), it becomes a \ncandidate.\n\nCandidate: Trying to become leader. Votes for itself, requests votes from other nodes.\n\nLeader: Won the election. Sends heartbeats to followers. Processes all writes.\n\nTHE ELECTION PROCESS:\n\nStep 1: Follower timeout\n─────────────────────────\nNode B hasn't heard from the leader in 200ms.\nNode B increments its \"term\" counter (logical clock for elections)\nNode B transitions to Candidate\nNode B votes for itself\n\nStep 2: Request votes\n─────────────────────────\nNode B sends RequestVote to Nodes A and C:\n\"I want to be leader for term 7.\n My last log entry is at index 42, term 6.\n Will you vote for me?\"\n\nStep 3: Other nodes respond\n─────────────────────────\nNode A: \"Your log is at least as up-to-date as mine. Yes, I'll vote for you.\"\nNode C: \"Your log is more current than mine. Yes.\"\n\nStep 4: B wins majority\n─────────────────────────\nB has 3 votes (including itself). 3 > N/2 (majority).\nB becomes leader for term 7.\nB immediately sends heartbeats to all nodes.\n\nStep 5: Normal operation\n─────────────────────────\nB sends heartbeats every ~50ms.\nA and C reset their election timeout each time they receive a heartbeat.\nIf they don't receive a heartbeat for 150-300ms, they start a new election.\nWhy the Log Freshness Check Matters\n\nA node only grants its vote if the candidate's log is at least as up-to-date as its own. This ensures the new leader has all committed entries.\n\nIf Node C has log entries [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] (5 committed)\nAnd Node B only has [1, 2, 3] (3 committed)\nNode B becomes candidate and asks C to vote\n\nC checks: \"B's log ends at index 3, mine ends at index 5. B is behind.\"\nC refuses to vote for B.\n\nB cannot win the election (it can get at most 1 vote — its own)\nThe leader must be someone who has at least entries up to index 5\n→ Data safety is preserved.\n\nTERMS - THE LOGICAL CLOCK:\n\nEach election attempt increments the term number. Terms are the logical clock of Raft:\n\nTerm 1: Node A is elected leader\nTerm 2: A fails, B is elected leader (after election)\nTerm 3: Network partition, C thinks B failed, starts election\n        B also starts re-election\n        C wins with 2 votes. Term 3.\nTerm 4: Normal operation under C\n\nA node always follows the highest term leader it knows about. If a node receives a message from a higher term, it immediately reverts to follower and updates its term. This prevents stale leaders from causing issues.\n\nPractical Leader Election With etcd\n\nIn production, you don't implement Raft yourself. You use etcd (or ZooKeeper, or Consul) which implements it for you.\n\njavascript\nconst { Etcd3 } = require('etcd3');\nconst client = new Etcd3();\n\nconst acquireLeadership = async (serviceId) => {\n    // Create a lease (will auto-expire if this process crashes)\n    const lease = client.lease(10); // 10 second TTL\n\n    try {\n        // Try to put our service ID as the leader key\n        // Only succeeds if the key doesn't exist (etcd's atomic compare-and-swap)\n        const election = await client.election('my-service-leader');\n        await election.campaign(serviceId);\n\n        console.log(`${serviceId} is now the leader`);\n\n        // Keep the lease alive (renew before it expires)\n        lease.keepaliveOnce();\n\n        // Listen for loss of leadership\n        election.observe().on('change', (leader) => {\n            if (leader !== serviceId) {\n                console.log(`Lost leadership to ${leader}`);\n                becomeFollower();\n            }\n        });\n\n    } catch (err) {\n        console.log(`Failed to become leader: ${err.message}`);\n        becomeFollower();\n    }\n};\n\n// The key insight: if the leader process crashes, the lease expires\n// after 10 seconds (the TTL), and another node can acquire leadership.\n// This is how failures are detected without manual intervention.\n\nTHE LEASE — HOW FAILURE DETECTION WORKS\n\nThe leader holds a lease — a time-limited ownership. The leader must continuously renew its lease before it expires. If the leader crashes, it can't \nrenew, the lease expires, and other nodes can compete to become the new leader.\n\nLeader alive:\n  [Leader] → renew lease every 5s → [etcd] → lease valid for 10s\n  [Other nodes] → \"lease is held, I'm a follower\"\n\nLeader crashes:\n  [Leader] ← CRASH\n  5s, 6s, 7s, 8s, 9s... no renewal\n  [etcd] → lease expires after 10s\n  [Other nodes] → \"lease expired! Time to elect a new leader\"\n\nThe TTL on the lease is your failure detection time. Shorter TTL = faster failure detection but more etcd traffic and more risk of false failures \n(network hiccup looks like a crash). Longer TTL = slower failure detection but more robust.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                                        LEADER ELECTION\nWhy You Need It\n\nMany distributed system components require exactly one \"leader\" to be active at a time:\n\n- Database primary: Exactly one node should accept writes to avoid conflicts\n- Kafka partition leader: Exactly one broker should handle produce/consume for each partition\n- Scheduled job coordinator: Exactly one instance should run the cron job (not all 10 instances)\n- Distributed lock manager: Exactly one node should grant locks at a time\n\nIf multiple nodes simultaneously believe they're the leader — split-brain — you get data corruption, duplicate job execution, or deadlocks.\n\nLEADER ELECTION is the algorithm by which distributed nodes agree on who is the leader, and detect when the leader fails so a new one can \nbe elected.\n\nREQUIREMENTS FOR A GOOD LEADER ELECTION ALGORITHM:\n\n- Safety: At most one leader at any time. Never two.\n- Liveness: Eventually, a leader is elected (the system doesn't get stuck)\n- Fault tolerance: If the leader fails, a new leader is elected\n- Freshness: The newly elected leader has the most up-to-date state (so it doesn't undo committed writes)\n\nThese requirements create tension. Safety and liveness are in tension during partitions (CAP theorem again). A system that ensures at most one \nleader (safety) might sacrifice liveness during partitions. A system that always elects a leader (liveness) might allow split-brain (sacrificing safety).\n\n                                                            THE RAFT ALGORITHM — HOW IT WORKS\n\nRaft is the dominant modern leader election algorithm. It powers etcd (Kubernetes' state store), CockroachDB, TiKV, and many others. It was \ndesigned specifically to be understandable (unlike Paxos, which is notoriously hard to understand).\n\nRaft has three node states:\n\nFollower: Default state. Follows the leader's instructions. If it doesn't hear from a leader for an election timeout (150-300ms), it becomes a \ncandidate.\n\nCandidate: Trying to become leader. Votes for itself, requests votes from other nodes.\n\nLeader: Won the election. Sends heartbeats to followers. Processes all writes.\n\nTHE ELECTION PROCESS:\n\nStep 1: Follower timeout\n─────────────────────────\nNode B hasn't heard from the leader in 200ms.\nNode B increments its \"term\" counter (logical clock for elections)\nNode B transitions to Candidate\nNode B votes for itself\n\nStep 2: Request votes\n─────────────────────────\nNode B sends RequestVote to Nodes A and C:\n\"I want to be leader for term 7.\n My last log entry is at index 42, term 6.\n Will you vote for me?\"\n\nStep 3: Other nodes respond\n─────────────────────────\nNode A: \"Your log is at least as up-to-date as mine. Yes, I'll vote for you.\"\nNode C: \"Your log is more current than mine. Yes.\"\n\nStep 4: B wins majority\n─────────────────────────\nB has 3 votes (including itself). 3 > N/2 (majority).\nB becomes leader for term 7.\nB immediately sends heartbeats to all nodes.\n\nStep 5: Normal operation\n─────────────────────────\nB sends heartbeats every ~50ms.\nA and C reset their election timeout each time they receive a heartbeat.\nIf they don't receive a heartbeat for 150-300ms, they start a new election.\nWhy the Log Freshness Check Matters\n\nA node only grants its vote if the candidate's log is at least as up-to-date as its own. This ensures the new leader has all committed entries.\n\nIf Node C has log entries [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] (5 committed)\nAnd Node B only has [1, 2, 3] (3 committed)\nNode B becomes candidate and asks C to vote\n\nC checks: \"B's log ends at index 3, mine ends at index 5. B is behind.\"\nC refuses to vote for B.\n\nB cannot win the election (it can get at most 1 vote — its own)\nThe leader must be someone who has at least entries up to index 5\n→ Data safety is preserved.\n\nTERMS - THE LOGICAL CLOCK:\n\nEach election attempt increments the term number. Terms are the logical clock of Raft:\n\nTerm 1: Node A is elected leader\nTerm 2: A fails, B is elected leader (after election)\nTerm 3: Network partition, C thinks B failed, starts election\n        B also starts re-election\n        C wins with 2 votes. Term 3.\nTerm 4: Normal operation under C\n\nA node always follows the highest term leader it knows about. If a node receives a message from a higher term, it immediately reverts to follower and updates its term. This prevents stale leaders from causing issues.\n\nPractical Leader Election With etcd\n\nIn production, you don't implement Raft yourself. You use etcd (or ZooKeeper, or Consul) which implements it for you.\n\njavascript\nconst { Etcd3 } = require('etcd3');\nconst client = new Etcd3();\n\nconst acquireLeadership = async (serviceId) => {\n    // Create a lease (will auto-expire if this process crashes)\n    const lease = client.lease(10); // 10 second TTL\n\n    try {\n        // Try to put our service ID as the leader key\n        // Only succeeds if the key doesn't exist (etcd's atomic compare-and-swap)\n        const election = await client.election('my-service-leader');\n        await election.campaign(serviceId);\n\n        console.log(`${serviceId} is now the leader`);\n\n        // Keep the lease alive (renew before it expires)\n        lease.keepaliveOnce();\n\n        // Listen for loss of leadership\n        election.observe().on('change', (leader) => {\n            if (leader !== serviceId) {\n                console.log(`Lost leadership to ${leader}`);\n                becomeFollower();\n            }\n        });\n\n    } catch (err) {\n        console.log(`Failed to become leader: ${err.message}`);\n        becomeFollower();\n    }\n};\n\n// The key insight: if the leader process crashes, the lease expires\n// after 10 seconds (the TTL), and another node can acquire leadership.\n// This is how failures are detected without manual intervention.\n\nTHE LEASE — HOW FAILURE DETECTION WORKS\n\nThe leader holds a lease — a time-limited ownership. The leader must continuously renew its lease before it expires. If the leader crashes, it can't \nrenew, the lease expires, and other nodes can compete to become the new leader.\n\nLeader alive:\n  [Leader] → renew lease every 5s → [etcd] → lease valid for 10s\n  [Other nodes] → \"lease is held, I'm a follower\"\n\nLeader crashes:\n  [Leader] ← CRASH\n  5s, 6s, 7s, 8s, 9s... no renewal\n  [etcd] → lease expires after 10s\n  [Other nodes] → \"lease expired! Time to elect a new leader\"\n\nThe TTL on the lease is your failure detection time. Shorter TTL = faster failure detection but more etcd traffic and more risk of false failures \n(network hiccup looks like a crash). Longer TTL = slower failure detection but more robust.",
